# KS-1199 — Wednesday's task for Ornith, VITEST, **TEST-ONLY MODE, ONE NEW TEST FILE** (written 09:25 on 2026-09-17)

Written from origin develop `fa887f382b212b8da4a0a4a556bacb05ea34daaa` (#1014's squash, KS-1176; its parent
`7e89318bc` is #1016's squash, KS-1072). The product file `Blockchain/Dev/services/api-gateway/src/routes/verification.ts`
(1479 lines, blob `28fb58343`, the #1016 gate's probe blob) was read at `:210-380` (the status mapping and
`makeFetchDocFromAnchorStore`) and `:470-780` (the `POST /api/documents/:id/verify` handler through its `res.json`). The
sibling test `ks1072-the-latest-anchor-selector-documents-a.test.ts` (blob `4ad1cdcd1`, 177 lines) was read whole; this
task copies its router-building shape. The #1016 gate verdict (P-1016-3, item 7 G-STATUS) and KS-1199 were read.
**PRE-MEASURED** in a scratch clone at the tip; see `## Premises (measured)`.

## THE MODE — read this twice

TEST-ONLY, ONE NEW FILE. Your diff contains EXACTLY ONE file: the NEW test file
`Blockchain/Dev/services/api-gateway/src/__tests__/ks1199-status-differing-anchor-tie-verdict.test.ts`, written as
`--- /dev/null` then `+++ b/…`, one `@@ -0,0 +1,113 @@` hunk, every line `+`.
**Do NOT modify `routes/verification.ts`**, and do NOT modify `ks1072-the-latest-anchor-selector-documents-a.test.ts`
(the reference test). `verification.ts` is in files[...] only so you can see the lines the new test reaches. The checker
plants the tamper named at the bottom into a clone of `verification.ts` and expects the three 🔴 cells RED and the 🟢
control GREEN, then expects all four GREEN at the untouched tip.

## What is wrong (one paragraph)

KS-1072 made the tier-2 latest-anchor selector break an equal `blockNumber` on the most recent `confirmedAt` (`:303-307`),
and since KS-1057 the selected row's `status` decides the verdict: the synthesised blob carries `status: latest.status`
(`:330`), only `confirmed` earns `persistedAnchored` (`:722`), and `verified` is `!isRevoked && hashValid &&
blockchainAnchored` (`:752`). But every ks1072 cell ties two `confirmed` rows and asserts `txHash` only. So the #1016
gate's G-STATUS mutant (a tie prefers the `confirmed` row) kept the whole api-gateway suite green while 14 of its rows
flipped a newer FAILED anchor back to on-chain `verified: true`. Nothing pins the verdict against that regression.
**This task:** a NEW file drives the REAL `createVerificationRoutes` router, with a stub anchor store that serves two
rows at the SAME `blockNumber` that differ in `status` and `confirmedAt`, and asserts the whole verdict
`[HTTP status, anchor-store reads, verified, verificationConfidence, blockchain.txHash]` — the newer row decides.

## Where (no product site changes — this list is informational; every line below STAYS)

- `:303` — (correct) `              const latest = anchors.slice().sort((a: any, b: any) => {` — the tier-2 selector. Stays.
- `:306` — (correct) `                return (Date.parse(b.confirmedAt) || 0) - (Date.parse(a.confirmedAt) || 0);` — the KS-1072 tiebreak the cells pin. The tamper replaces this line. Stays.
- `:330` — (correct) `                  status: latest.status,` — the selected row's status carried into the blob. Stays.
- `:722` — (correct) `      (persistedStatus === 'confirmed' || (persistedStatus == null && (doc as any)._source !== 'anchor_store')), // KS-1073: carve-out tier-1 only; anchor-store rows are never statusless` — only `confirmed` earns on-chain. Stays.
- `:752` — (correct) `      verified: !isRevoked && hashValid && blockchainAnchored,` — the verdict the cells assert. Stays.

## The test — CREATE THE NEW FILE `Blockchain/Dev/services/api-gateway/src/__tests__/ks1199-status-differing-anchor-tie-verdict.test.ts`

**Harness:** `vitest run` inside `Blockchain/Dev/services/api-gateway` (`package.json` scripts.test is `vitest`;
`vitest.config.ts`: globals, node environment, `setupFiles: ['./vitest.setup.ts']`). To run this file alone:
`npx vitest run src/__tests__/ks1199-status-differing-anchor-tie-verdict.test.ts`.

The router deps object is the ks1072 reference test's own (the same keys and the same `vi.fn` stubs, some keys joined
onto one line). Two stub servers on `127.0.0.1:0`: the anchor store answers `/api/anchors/document/doc-ks1199` with
`anchorStoreRows` and counts each read in `anchorStoreHits`; originate 404s everything, so tier 1 never answers and
tier 2 is the tier that decides. `process.env.ANCHORING_SERVICE_URL` points the handler's live scan at the anchor stub
(which 404s it, so the persisted row decides and nothing leaves loopback) and is restored in `afterAll`.
`verdictFor(rows)` serves the rows, zeroes the counter, POSTs `/api/documents/doc-ks1199/verify` with the matching
`contentHash`, and answers the five-element verdict array. No network beyond 127.0.0.1, no database.

**Reproduce the body below EXACTLY as written.** It is 113 lines. Do not shorten it, do not replace any part with a
comment, do not invent a helper, do not rename a variable, do not collapse the cells into a loop or an `it.each`. Every
name a cell reads (`verdictFor`, `row`, `JAN`, `JUN`, `OLD_TX`, `NEW_TX`) is declared ABOVE the first cell, and every
name those read (`DOC_ID`, `CONTENT_HASH`, `jsonHead`, `gatewayPort`, `anchorStoreRows`, `anchorStoreHits`) is declared
above them. **The file contains no backslash anywhere, no template literal, no dollar sign and no double quote; keep it
that way.** Every string is single-quoted and contains no quote character, so no escape is needed anywhere. The two
emoji in the `it(` titles are written as the characters themselves, never as an escape.

```ts
// KS-1199 (the #1016 gate P-1016-3 on KS-1072): every ks1072 cell ties two CONFIRMED anchors and asserts txHash only, so a
// tiebreak that preferred the confirmed row passed 424 of 424 cells. These cells pin the VERDICT on a status-differing tie.
import { describe, it, expect, beforeAll, afterAll, vi } from 'vitest';
import express from 'express';
import http from 'node:http';
import type { Server } from 'node:http';
import type { AddressInfo } from 'node:net';
import type { RequestHandler } from 'express';

const DOC_ID = 'doc-ks1199';
const CONTENT_HASH = 'c'.repeat(64);
const JAN = '2026-01-01T00:00:00Z';
const JUN = '2026-06-01T00:00:00Z';
const OLD_TX = 'a'.repeat(64);
const NEW_TX = 'b'.repeat(64);
const jsonHead = { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' };
const priorAnchoringUrl = process.env.ANCHORING_SERVICE_URL;

let gateway: Server;
let anchorServer: Server;
let originate: Server;
let gatewayPort = 0;
let anchorStoreRows: Array<Record<string, unknown>> = []; // the tier-2 rows the stub anchor store serves, in the order listed
let anchorStoreHits = 0; // reads of this document from the stub anchor store in the current cell: the tier-2 witness

/** One anchor row at the SAME blockNumber as every other row in this file, so only the tiebreak separates them. */
function row(status: string, confirmedAt: string, txHash: string): Record<string, unknown> {
  return { blockNumber: 100, confirmedAt, transaction_hash: txHash, status, contentHash: CONTENT_HASH };
}

/** Verifies the document over the real router with these rows; answers [status, anchor-store hits, verified, confidence, txHash]. */
async function verdictFor(rows: Array<Record<string, unknown>>): Promise<unknown[]> {
  anchorStoreRows = rows;
  anchorStoreHits = 0;
  const url = 'http://127.0.0.1:' + String(gatewayPort) + '/api/documents/' + DOC_ID + '/verify';
  const res = await fetch(url, { method: 'POST', headers: jsonHead, body: JSON.stringify({ contentHash: CONTENT_HASH, purpose: 'test' }) });
  const body = (await res.json()) as { verified?: unknown; verificationConfidence?: unknown; blockchain?: { txHash?: unknown } };
  return [res.status, anchorStoreHits, body.verified, body.verificationConfidence, body.blockchain?.txHash];
}

beforeAll(async () => {
  anchorServer = http.createServer((req, res) => {
    if (req.url === '/api/anchors/document/' + DOC_ID) {
      anchorStoreHits += 1;
      res.writeHead(200, jsonHead);
      res.end(JSON.stringify({ data: { anchors: anchorStoreRows } }));
      return;
    }
    res.writeHead(404, jsonHead); res.end('{}');
  });
  anchorServer.listen(0, '127.0.0.1');
  await new Promise<void>((r) => anchorServer.once('listening', () => r()));
  const anchorUrl = 'http://127.0.0.1:' + String((anchorServer.address() as AddressInfo).port);
  process.env.ANCHORING_SERVICE_URL = anchorUrl; // the live scan answers 404 on loopback, never off-host

  originate = http.createServer((_req, res) => { res.writeHead(404, jsonHead); res.end('{}'); }); // tier 1 never answers
  originate.listen(0, '127.0.0.1');
  await new Promise<void>((r) => originate.once('listening', () => r()));
  const originateUrl = 'http://127.0.0.1:' + String((originate.address() as AddressInfo).port);

  const { createVerificationRoutes } = await import('../routes/verification');
  const mockBodyParser: RequestHandler = express.json({ limit: '1mb' });
  const app = express();
  app.use(createVerificationRoutes({
    authenticateToken: () => (_req, _res, next) => next(), mockBodyParser,
    query: vi.fn(async () => ({ rows: [] })) as never,
    isDbAvailable: () => false, log: () => undefined,
    redisService: {
      getPendingDocument: vi.fn(async () => null), deletePendingDocument: vi.fn(async () => undefined), getAllDocumentTypes: vi.fn(async () => []),
      getAllWorkflowInstances: vi.fn(async () => []), getNotificationSettings: vi.fn(async () => ({})), getRejectedDocument: vi.fn(async () => null),
      setRejectedDocument: vi.fn(async () => undefined), getWorkflowDocumentMapping: vi.fn(async () => null),
      getWorkflowInstance: vi.fn(async () => null), setWorkflowInstance: vi.fn(async () => undefined),
    } as never,
    services: { originate: { url: originateUrl }, anchoring: { url: anchorUrl } } as never,
    memWorkflowToDocumentMap: new Map(), memRejectedDocuments: new Map(),
    dbSaveRejection: vi.fn(async () => undefined), ADMIN_ROLES: ['ADMIN', 'admin'],
    enforceDocumentTypeRules: vi.fn(async () => ({ ok: true, docType: {} })) as never,
    createWorkflowInstanceIfRequired: vi.fn(async () => ({ gated: false })) as never,
    meetsVerificationLevel: () => true,
  }));
  gateway = app.listen(0, '127.0.0.1');
  await new Promise<void>((r) => gateway.once('listening', () => r()));
  gatewayPort = (gateway.address() as AddressInfo).port;
});

afterAll(async () => {
  if (priorAnchoringUrl === undefined) delete process.env.ANCHORING_SERVICE_URL; else process.env.ANCHORING_SERVICE_URL = priorAnchoringUrl;
  await new Promise<void>((r) => gateway.close(() => r()));
  await new Promise<void>((r) => anchorServer.close(() => r()));
  await new Promise<void>((r) => originate.close(() => r()));
});

describe('KS-1199 — an equal-blockNumber tie whose rows differ in status: the newer row decides the verdict', () => {
  it('🔴 KS-1199 — confirmed January listed first, failed June second: not verified, off-chain-only', async () => {
    const answer = await verdictFor([row('confirmed', JAN, OLD_TX), row('failed', JUN, NEW_TX)]);
    expect(answer, 'the newer failed anchor decides').toEqual([200, 1, false, 'off-chain-only', NEW_TX]);
  });

  it('🔴 KS-1199 — failed June listed first, confirmed January second: still not verified', async () => {
    const answer = await verdictFor([row('failed', JUN, NEW_TX), row('confirmed', JAN, OLD_TX)]);
    expect(answer, 'the newer failed anchor decides whatever the order').toEqual([200, 1, false, 'off-chain-only', NEW_TX]);
  });

  it('🔴 KS-1199 — confirmed January listed first, submitted June second: pending, not verified', async () => {
    const answer = await verdictFor([row('confirmed', JAN, OLD_TX), row('submitted', JUN, NEW_TX)]);
    expect(answer, 'the newer in-flight anchor decides').toEqual([200, 1, false, 'pending-onchain', NEW_TX]);
  });

  it('🟢 KS-1199 control — failed January listed first, confirmed June second: verified on-chain', async () => {
    const answer = await verdictFor([row('failed', JAN, OLD_TX), row('confirmed', JUN, NEW_TX)]);
    expect(answer, 'the newer confirmed anchor decides').toEqual([200, 1, true, 'on-chain', NEW_TX]);
  });
});
```

Why each cell is what it is (all four rows sit at `blockNumber: 100`, so only the tiebreak separates them):
- **🔴 confirmed January first, failed June second:** the June row is newer, so it is selected; `failed` answers
  `[200, 1, false, 'off-chain-only', NEW_TX]`.
- **🔴 failed June first, confirmed January second:** the same pair in the other order. It is green even with NO
  tiebreak (the first-listed row wins), so it goes red only when a tie PREFERS a status — the exact G-STATUS shape.
- **🔴 confirmed January first, submitted June second:** the newer row is in flight: `[200, 1, false, 'pending-onchain',
  NEW_TX]`. It pins the verdict on a tie that is not `failed` as well.
- Under the tamper (the tiebreak prefers the `confirmed` row, then the date — the gate's G-STATUS) all three 🔴 cells
  fail **by assertion**: `expected [ 200, 1, true, 'on-chain', …(1) ] to deeply equal [ 200, 1, false, …(2) ]` — the
  older confirmed anchor verifies.
- **🟢 control:** failed January first, confirmed June second → `[200, 1, true, 'on-chain', NEW_TX]`. Green under the
  tamper (the newer row IS the confirmed one). It proves the harness can produce `verified: true` through tier 2, so a
  🔴 `false` is the selector's choice and not a harness that never verifies; the `1` in every array proves the anchor
  store was read exactly once, i.e. tier 2 answered.

## Red cells

- 🔴 KS-1199 — confirmed January listed first, failed June second: not verified, off-chain-only
- 🔴 KS-1199 — failed June listed first, confirmed January second: still not verified
- 🔴 KS-1199 — confirmed January listed first, submitted June second: pending, not verified

## Tamper (test-only mode — the checker plants this in a clone to prove your cells can go red)

line: 306
from: `                return (Date.parse(b.confirmedAt) || 0) - (Date.parse(a.confirmedAt) || 0);`
to: `                return ((b.status === 'confirmed' ? 1 : 0) - (a.status === 'confirmed' ? 1 : 0)) || ((Date.parse(b.confirmedAt) || 0) - (Date.parse(a.confirmedAt) || 0));`
statement_ok: the `from` line is a whole `return …;` statement, the last statement of the comparator body that opens at `:303` (`anchors.slice().sort((a: any, b: any) => {`). `:304` computes `byBlock` and `:305` returns it only when it is non-zero, so on an equal `blockNumber` control always reaches `:306`; the next line `:307` `})[0];` only closes the arrow function and takes the first element — it cannot veto the returned order. Read at the tip 09:2x. It is the gate's G-STATUS (a tie prefers `confirmed`) as one line. Project tsc rc 0.

## Output

Exactly ONE ```diff block containing ONLY the new test file: `--- /dev/null` with NO leading space, then
`+++ b/Blockchain/Dev/services/api-gateway/src/__tests__/ks1199-status-differing-anchor-tie-verdict.test.ts`, then
`@@ -0,0 +1,113 @@`, then the 113 lines above, each prefixed with `+`. Blank lines are a bare `+`. No hunk for
`verification.ts` or any other file.

